<problem>
<!-- <title>stability of a given discrete equilibrium, f=ax+bx^2, variant 2</title> -->

<setup>

<select name="x"> x y z u v w p alpha beta gamma</select>
<select name="n"> n t</select>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from=".1" to=" 1.9" step=" .1" exclude="1" />
<selectFromSequence name="d" from=".5" to=" 2" step=" .5" />
<selectFromSequence name="i" from="0" to="3" />
<selectFromSequence name="j" from="max(0, 1-$i)" to="3" />

<number name="B">2^$i *5^$j </number>
<number name="b">1/$B </number>

<number name="E">$a *$B </number>
<number name="alpha">1+$a </number>
<number name="two_b">2*$b </number>
<number name="f_prime_E">$alpha +2*$b *$E </number>
<number name="abs_f_prime_E">abs($f_prime_E )</number>

</setup>


<p>The discrete dynamical system below has an equilibrium <m>E=$E </m>.  Determine if the equilibrium point, <m>E=$E </m>,  is stable or unstable.  Hint: Use the stability theorem for discrete systems.</p>
<md>
    <mrow>$x _{ $n +1}\amp = $alpha $x _$n  + $b $x _$n ^2</mrow>
  <mrow>$x _0\amp = $d</mrow>
</md>

<m>E = $E </m> is 
<answer maxNumAttempts="1">
<choiceInput inline="true">
<choice>stable</choice>
<choice credit="1">unstable</choice>
</choiceInput>
</answer>

<solution>
<p>The dynamical system is already in function iteration form, with <m>$x _{ $n +1}  = f($x _$n )</m>
for <m>f($x ) = $alpha x + $b $x ^2.</m>  The stability is determined by the derivative of <m>f</m> evaluated at the equilibria.</p>

<p>Since
<me>f'($x ) = $alpha  + $two_b $x ,</me>
and 
<me>f'($E ) = $alpha  + $two_b ($E ) = $f_prime_E ,</me>
we see that
<me>\left|f'($E ) \right| = $abs_f_prime_E  \gt  1.</me>
The equilibrium <m>E=$E </m> is unstable.
</p>

</solution>


</problem>